namespace Dakota {

/** \page IteratorExecutorDesign IteratorExecutor Design Intent

\section IteratorExecutorPurpose Purpose

IteratorExecutor is the low-level execution mechanism for iterator
orchestration in parallel runtime contexts. It is the class that knows how
to do the concrete work of preparing, partitioning, initializing, running,
and tearing down iterator execution under Dakota's communicator and
scheduling rules.

If StudyRuntime is the runtime-facing abstraction and IteratorContext is the
per-subordinate execution handle, IteratorExecutor is the engine behind
those abstractions.

\section IteratorExecutorResponsibilities Responsibilities

IteratorExecutor owns the mechanics of subordinate iterator execution,
including:

<ul>
<li>tracking the relevant parallel configuration iterator,</li>
<li>estimating partition bounds through configure(),</li>
<li>partitioning ranks for iterator execution,</li>
<li>initializing a child iterator sufficiently to run,</li>
<li>setting and freeing iterator communicators,</li>
<li>running iterators and scheduling batches of iterator jobs, and</li>
<li>maintaining scheduling-related state such as iterator server identity,
message-passing mode, and communicator rank/size information.</li>
</ul>

This class is where most of the real MPI-aware execution behavior still
lives today.

\section IteratorExecutorStable Stable Intent

The stable intent is that IteratorExecutor remains a mechanism object,
concerned with how iterator execution happens rather than what a study
means.

It is expected to remain the place where communicator-partitioning and
iterator-scheduling mechanics are implemented, even if the surrounding
runtime abstractions change shape.

\section IteratorExecutorExpectedChange Expected Evolution

The surface through which IteratorExecutor is accessed may change over time.
In particular, more policy and lifecycle coordination may migrate upward
into StudyRuntime or be packaged more cleanly through IteratorContext.

What is less likely to change is the need for some mechanism object that
encodes Dakota's concrete iterator-execution protocol. IteratorExecutor is
that object today.

\section IteratorExecutorNonGoal Non-Goals

IteratorExecutor is not intended to define user-facing API semantics. It is
also not intended to be the conceptual center of library-mode execution.
Those roles belong more naturally to StudyRuntime and the surrounding design
notes.

For future developers, the important thing to remember is that
IteratorExecutor should be read as an execution mechanism, not as an
independent semantic abstraction.

\section IteratorExecutorSeeAlso See Also

See \ref RuntimeExecutionDesign for the subsystem overview.

*/

} // namespace Dakota
